# Metro–Bike Coupling Graph Convolutional Network (MBC-GCN)

Code accompanying *"When Do Metro Flows Improve Shared-Bike Demand Forecasts?
A Cross-Modal Graph Learning Framework"*.

The pipeline runs in four stages, mirroring the structure of the paper.

```
raw AFC + bike logs
        |
        v
  01_build_station_panel.py      ->  5-minute station-time panel per buffer radius
        |
        +--> 02_coupling_diagnostics.py   -> Table 1 (Granger), Table 2 (NB-GLM)
        |
        +--> 03_forecast_global.py --resolution 5min  -> Table 6, Fig. 7
        |
        v
  01b_aggregate_hourly.py        ->  hourly station-time panel per buffer radius
        |
        +--> 03_forecast_global.py --resolution 1h    -> Table 4, Table 5, Fig. 6
        |
        +--> 04_forecast_station_level.py             -> Table 7, Fig. 8
```

## Installation

```bash
python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

## Data

The Shanghai AFC and dockless-bike records were provided by our partner
companies and cannot be released publicly. Access may be granted on request;
please contact the corresponding author. To run the code, place your own copies
under `data/` with the following structure and column names.

| File | Required columns |
| --- | --- |
| `bike20221212-20230101.csv` | `BICYCLE_ID`, `DATA_TIME`, `LONGITUDE`, `LATITUDE`, `LOCK_STATUS` |
| `metro_station_coordinates.csv` | `stationnames`, `lon`, `lat` |
| `station_info.csv` | `FSTR_LINEID`, `FSTR_STATIONID`, `FSTR_STATIONNAME` |
| `afc_5min/HIST_STO_5MFLOW_5M_YYYYMMDD.CSV` | `FSTR_LINEID`, `FSTR_STATIONID`, `FDT_STARTTIME`, `FDT_ENDTIME`, `FINT_IN`, `FINT_OUT` |

Analysis window: 12–31 December 2022 (20 days).

## Stage 1 — Travel-chain reconstruction

```bash
python src/01_build_station_panel.py \
    --bike-csv       data/bike20221212-20230101.csv \
    --station-coords data/metro_station_coordinates.csv \
    --station-info   data/station_info.csv \
    --flow-dir       data/afc_5min \
    --out-root       outputs/panel \
    --buffers 50 100 200 500
```

Lock/unlock events are mirrored with a one-second offset and inverted lock state
so that point events become continuous intervals; midnight anchors are added at
both ends of each bicycle's day; riding and parking segments are classified from
consecutive lock states; segments shorter than 10 s are discarded. Bike events
are then matched to metro station exits by Manhattan distance in Web Mercator
metres and aggregated onto the 5-minute AFC grid as `ride_in`, `ride_out` and
`delta_bikes`.

Output: `outputs/panel/{50,100,200,500}m/merged_YYYYMMDD.csv`.

## Stage 1b — Hourly aggregation

```bash
python src/01b_aggregate_hourly.py --panel-root outputs/panel --buffers 50 100 200 500
```

Sums the four count columns within each (line, station, date, hour) cell.

Output: `outputs/panel/{50,100,200,500}m/20days_data_1h.csv`, with columns
`FINT_IN_1h`, `FINT_OUT_1h`, `ride_in_1h`, `ride_out_1h` plus the station
identifiers, coordinates, `date` and `hour`.

The original notebook first concatenated all 20 daily files into an intermediate
`20days_data.csv` and grouped afterwards. This script groups each day separately
and concatenates the results, which is arithmetically identical because `date`
is a grouping key and no hour spans two days, but keeps peak memory
proportional to one day instead of the whole window.

## Stage 2 — Coupling diagnostics

```bash
python src/02_coupling_diagnostics.py --panel-root outputs/panel --test all
```

Runs bidirectional Granger causality tests (maximum lag 5) and station
fixed-effect Negative Binomial GLMs for each buffer radius. The NB-GLM
coefficient profile across buffers is what identifies 100 m as the peak-coupling
catchment used downstream.

Output: `outputs/diagnostics/granger_5min.csv`, `outputs/diagnostics/nb_glm_5min.csv`.

## Stage 3 — Network-wide forecasting

```bash
# Scenario A: 5-minute resolution (Table 6, Fig. 7)
python src/03_forecast_global.py --resolution 5min \
    --panel-dir outputs/panel/100m --out-dir outputs/forecast_5min

# Scenario B: 1-hour resolution, both input settings (Table 4)
python src/03_forecast_global.py --resolution 1h --features ar \
    --hourly-csv outputs/panel/200m/20days_data_1h.csv \
    --out-dir outputs/forecast_1h_ar

python src/03_forecast_global.py --resolution 1h --features semi_ar \
    --hourly-csv outputs/panel/200m/20days_data_1h.csv \
    --out-dir outputs/forecast_1h_semi_ar
```

`--features ar` supplies bike flows and calendar features only;
`--features semi_ar` adds metro entries and exits.

## Stage 4 — Station-level forecasting

```bash
python src/04_forecast_station_level.py --mode train \
    --hourly-csv outputs/panel/200m/20days_data_1h.csv \
    --out-dir outputs/station_level --nodes 95 144 239 298

# redraw figures from saved checkpoints without retraining
python src/04_forecast_station_level.py --mode plot \
    --hourly-csv outputs/panel/200m/20days_data_1h.csv \
    --out-dir outputs/station_level
```

Models are trained on the full station graph, but the loss and the reported
metrics are restricted to one station at a time. Node indices follow the
alphabetical order of station names in the hourly panel, so they must be
re-checked if the station list changes.

## Reproducibility notes and known gaps

All four scripts have been run end to end on a synthetic dataset with the
schema documented above, so the pipeline itself executes without errors on
pandas 2.x and torch 2.x. The following are documented so that anyone rerunning
the code knows where the published numbers cannot be regenerated end to end from
this repository alone.


   ```

1. **Buffer radius used by the forecasting stages.** The paper states that node
   features aggregate lock/unlock events within 100 m of each station exit. The
   5-minute scenario does read the 100 m panel, but the hourly scenarios were run
   against the 200 m panel. The default paths reflect what was actually run;
   change `--hourly-csv` to the 100 m directory if the intention was 100 m
   throughout.

2. **Table 4 / Table 5 / Figure 6.** The AR and Semi-AR rows are produced by
   running stage 3 twice with different `--features` values. The attention-
   augmented variants reported in the Semi-AR block ("MBC-GCN + Attn",
   "ST-GCN + Attn") use the attention module implemented in stage 4; the
   network-wide script implements attention only in the 5-minute models, so the
   hourly attention variants would need to be run with the stage 4 backbone
   evaluated over all stations rather than one.

3. **Figure 4 (kernel density of bike activity)** was produced in ArcGIS and has
   no counterpart in this repository.

4. **Non-determinism.** Optuna searches, model initialisation and dropout are
   seeded only for the station-level sampler (`RANDOM_SEED = 20260301`). Metric
   values will vary by a small margin between runs.



